vscode: the Problems panel after a build task
vimrc: set makeprg=cat\ tsc-output.txt errorformat=%f(%l\\,%c):\ %m hidden
open: README.md

@brief
`:make` runs the program named in `makeprg`, captures its output,
parses it with `errorformat`, and jumps to the first error. In a
TypeScript project `makeprg` would be `npx tsc --noEmit`. There is no
compiler in this dojo, so for this lesson it is `cat tsc-output.txt`:
saved output, delivered the way a real build would deliver it. Nothing
on the quickfix side changes; only where the lines came from.

Four errors this time. Fix each and `:cn`. If vim shows the output and
asks you to press Enter, press it. In a real project you would run
`:make` again at the end and expect an empty list; here the file is
static, so there is no point.

@keys
| `:make` | run `makeprg`, errors into the quickfix list, jump to the first |
| `:make!` | the same without jumping |
| `makeprg` | the build command; the default is `make` |

@hints
`:make`. The first error is a misspelling of `exception`; the cursor is
on it, and `ciw` with the right spelling fixes it.
--
The second is a duplicated `status` line in the entity: `dd`. The
third is a call with one argument too many; the cursor is on the extra
argument, and the comma and space before it have to go too.
--
From the extra argument, `2h` puts you on the comma and `dt)` deletes
up to the closing paren. The fourth is `response.body` where the type
has `data`: `ciw`. `:wq`.

@solution
    :make<Enter>
    ciw exception <Esc> :w<Enter> :cn<Enter>
    dd :w<Enter> :cn<Enter>
    2h dt) :w<Enter> :cn<Enter>
    ciw data <Esc> :wq<Enter>

The loop this sets up is the one that makes vim feel like an IDE
without being one: `:make`, fix, `:cn`, fix, `:make` again until the
list is empty. `makeprg` and `errorformat` are both options, so they
can be set per filetype, and vim ships compiler definitions for many
tools: `:compiler tsc` sets both for TypeScript in one go, and
`:compiler` with no argument lists what is available.

`:make` runs the whole program and waits for it. On a large project
that is a pause you notice; on a small one it is nothing. Either way
the result is the same list you have been using all chapter, and every
command you know works on it.

@start README.md
# Orders service

Cancelling an order refunds it through `PaymentService.sendRefund`, which
is named after the gateway's `/sendRefund` endpoint. A refund the gateway
rejects raises `REFUND_FAILED`. Refund events go out on the
`payment.sendRefund` routing key, which the ledger service consumes.

@start src/domain/customer/customer.provider.ts
import { injectable, inject } from 'tsyringe';
import { exception } from '../../configuration/errors';
import { CustomerRepository } from './customer.repository';

@injectable()
export class CustomerProvider {
  constructor(
    @inject(CustomerRepository) protected customerRepository: CustomerRepository,
  ) {}

  public async findOne(id: string) {
    const customer = await this.customerRepository.findOne({ where: { id } });

    if (!customer) {
      throw exeption('CUSTOMER_NOT_FOUND');
    }

    return customer;
  }
}

@expected src/domain/customer/customer.provider.ts
import { injectable, inject } from 'tsyringe';
import { exception } from '../../configuration/errors';
import { CustomerRepository } from './customer.repository';

@injectable()
export class CustomerProvider {
  constructor(
    @inject(CustomerRepository) protected customerRepository: CustomerRepository,
  ) {}

  public async findOne(id: string) {
    const customer = await this.customerRepository.findOne({ where: { id } });

    if (!customer) {
      throw exception('CUSTOMER_NOT_FOUND');
    }

    return customer;
  }
}

